{
  "profile": "operator.receipt/1",
  "description": "The receipt a node signs to say it carried a submission-set. It is the operator's own statement, so a third party must be able to check it without asking the node anything — which needs the canonical bytes pinned.",
  "rules": {
    "operator_id": "op: + base64(SHA3-256(operator_public_key)[0:16]), STANDARD alphabet with NO padding. Sixteen bytes, not thirty-two: the id identifies, it does not authenticate — the signature does that.",
    "canonicalization": "JCS over the receipt WITHOUT its signature field. Note the contrast with level1-binding/1, which signs insertion-ordered bytes: here the keys come out sorted (issued_at, operator_id, receipt_version, rules_version, submission_set_id). Two artifacts of the same system, two canonicalizations, and each vector pins its own so neither can be guessed from the other.",
    "signature": "ML-DSA-65 (FIPS 204) detached over those bytes, base64 STANDARD.",
    "verification": "Rebuild the no-signature view, canonicalize with JCS, verify the detached signature against the operator public key, and confirm operator_id derives from that same key — otherwise a valid signature from an unrelated key would pass as this operator."
  },
